## Result
The objects are memcg_alloc_slab_cgroups()'s obj_cgroup vectors (mm/memcontrol.c): with
kmem accounting, every new slab page of an accounted cache gets a vector of one pointer
per object, here buffer_head's (39 per page, 312 bytes, so kmalloc-512) as the page cache
of written data grows. About 600 per 100 MiB written, the same on eCryptfs and on plain
ext4 (the soak's 0.72 MB/s gives 4.5/s), all freed when the file's page cache goes (file
deleted, or the page cache reclaimed). In the soak all of the growth went with the
recording (section 2), at the rate its write rate gives. Not gc573: the driver allocates
a few objects per buffer at REQBUFS (src/gc573_vb2.c) and nothing per frame. Recording
into a directory outside eCryptfs does not change it.

For the soak's memory check this means SUnreclaim grows with the recording on any file
system. A leak check has to compare SUnreclaim before OBS starts with SUnreclaim after
OBS has exited and the recording is deleted.
